# Prove that a consumable a character refuses to use stays in the pack, and
# that one he does use still leaves it. This is the oracle for inc-bp2.
#
#   tools/headless.sh tools/keys/consumable-abort.keys 5
#
# THE SEED MATTERS AND IT MUST BE 5. The refusal this script needs is only
# offered after a Will save: Item::ReadScroll (src/Magic.cpp) prints "This
# scroll is powerful, and possibly beyond you!", rolls SavingThrow(WILL,15),
# and asks "Stop reading?" only when that roll succeeds. Most seeds fail the
# roll, and then the scroll is simply read, which measures nothing. Of seeds 1
# to 8 only 5 and 7 make it; 5 is the one this script and
# tools/check_consumable_abort.sh use. Changing ANY key before the read moves
# the die rolls under it, so a seed that works today stops working if this
# script gains or loses a keystroke -- re-hunt the seed rather than assuming.
# tools/check_consumable_abort.sh reports a run whose prompt never appeared as
# INCONCLUSIVE, not as a pass and not as a failure.
#
# Wizard mode supplies the two consumables, because neither can be walked to.
# [e] Item Acquisition opens TextTerm::AcquisitionPrompt (src/Term.cpp), whose
# list has NO menu letters and NO '>' marker, so neither @choose nor @cursorto
# can reach a row in it -- counting DOWN is the only way in. That makes the two
# counts below fragile: they are positions in the list the resource compiler
# builds from lib/, and adding a potion or a scroll effect moves everything
# after it. @expect nails each one to the name it is supposed to have landed
# on, so a drifted count stops the run instead of quietly acquiring the
# neighbour, and the check script re-reads both names out of the pack.
#
#   potions  are the first glyph category, so no RIGHT is needed.
#     DOWN*4      Potion of Healing        -- the control: no target prompt,
#                                             quaffing it must still spend it.
#   scrolls  are one RIGHT away.
#     DOWN*116    Scroll of Wizard Sight   -- level 5 and not EF_STAPLE, so
#                                             sLevel is 9 and beats any Decipher
#                                             a 1st-level barbarian can have,
#                                             which is what makes the "powerful"
#                                             branch fire at all. qval is 0, so
#                                             reading it asks for no target.
#
# An acquired item that will not stack lands in the In-the-Air slot and opens
# the inventory manager, which refuses to close while that slot is full; the
# two @while lines stow it and leave. An item that stacks never opens it, and
# then both lines fire zero times.
#
# Three pack dumps. The comparison between them is the whole test:
#
#   pack-before   3 Scrolls of Wizard Sight, 2 Potions of Healing.
#   pack-refused  after answering yes to "Stop reading?". BOTH counts MUST be
#                 unchanged: nothing was read, so nothing was spent.
#   pack-quaffed  after drinking one Potion of Healing. The potion count MUST
#                 have dropped. Without this half, a build that never consumes
#                 anything would pass.

@include chargen.keys

# --- two Potions of Healing ------------------------------------------------
w
@until "Wizard Mode Options" y
@choose "Item Acquisition"
DOWN*4
@expect "Potion of Healing"
ENTER
@while "In the Air':Potion" s
@while "Ready Hand" ESC

w
@until "Wizard Mode Options" y
@choose "Item Acquisition"
DOWN*4
ENTER
@while "In the Air':Potion" s
@while "Ready Hand" ESC

# --- three Scrolls of Wizard Sight -----------------------------------------
w
@until "Wizard Mode Options" y
@choose "Item Acquisition"
RIGHT
DOWN*116
@expect "Scroll of Wizard Sight"
ENTER
@while "In the Air':Scroll" s
@while "Ready Hand" ESC

w
@until "Wizard Mode Options" y
@choose "Item Acquisition"
RIGHT
DOWN*116
ENTER
@while "In the Air':Scroll" s
@while "Ready Hand" ESC

w
@until "Wizard Mode Options" y
@choose "Item Acquisition"
RIGHT
DOWN*116
ENTER
@while "In the Air':Scroll" s
@while "Ready Hand" ESC

i
@dump:pack-before
ESC

# --- refuse to read a scroll ------------------------------------------------
# The two use menus are ordinary LMenus, so they DO carry letters and @choose
# reads the one the game printed. Do not go back to a hardcoded [a] here: the
# adventurer starting kit already puts several potions in the pack, the menu
# is MENU_SORTED, and [a] is whichever of them sorts first -- which is how an
# earlier version of this script drank a Potion of See Invisibility and
# measured nothing. @choose matches from the START of the entry, and
# Player::UseItemMenu writes an item held in a container as "(in pack) 3
# Scrolls of ...", so the count is part of the name it must be given. Both
# counts below are the counts at that moment in a PASSING run; in a failing
# run they are still right, because nothing has been spent yet when either
# menu opens.
r
@choose "(in pack) 3 Scrolls of Wizard Sight"
@dump:offer         # "Stop reading? [yn]" MUST be on this screen
y                   # yes, stop -- nothing is read
i
@dump:pack-refused
ESC

# --- drink a potion, which must still spend it ------------------------------
q
@choose "(in pack) 2 Potions of Healing"
i
@dump:pack-quaffed
ESC

@quit
